{"text": " You wake up in 1,500 in a market town in Flanders. It's late autumn. The light is going and the church bell starts to ring. Did you know that this bell has a name and the name is Curfew? It comes from the old French for cover the fire. When you hear it, you bank the hearth and the day is over. Here's your evening. Work stops at sunset. A tallow candle made from sheep fat, smokes and stinks, and it costs too much to burn for long. So most nights you use a rush light, a dried rush dipped in grease, depending on its length at last a few minutes or an hour at most. Bees waxes for the church and the rich. What does one hour of light cost you? One ordinary bulb's worth for one hour. An economist William Nordhaus worked it out. An ancient Babylon about 41 hours of work and by 1800 in America, still more than five. In more than 3,000 years, the price barely moved. So what is light to you? A luxury. You make it yourself and you make it last. For 300 years, the dark winds then a man in London sets the air on fire.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.3, "text": " You wake up in 1,500 in a market town in Flanders.", "tokens": [50364, 509, 6634, 493, 294, 502, 11, 7526, 294, 257, 2142, 3954, 294, 479, 1661, 433, 13, 50560], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1237173792141587, "compression_ratio": 1.5745454545454545, "no_speech_prob": 0.020608730614185333, "words": [{"word": " You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.14, "probability": 0.5874795317649841}, {"word": " wake", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.34, "probability": 0.9902666211128235}, {"word": " up", "start": 0.34, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.99815434217453}, {"word": " in", "start": 0.58, "end": 0.72, "probability": 0.9914693236351013}, {"word": " 1", "start": 0.72, "end": 0.86, "probability": 0.33346807956695557}, {"word": ",500", "start": 0.86, "end": 1.54, "probability": 0.9912363886833191}, {"word": " in", "start": 1.54, "end": 1.9, "probability": 0.9326485991477966}, {"word": " a", "start": 1.9,